ing prophages. Most intriguingly, all of these 132 viral spacers matched to temperate bacteriophage\/prophages capable of inserting into the host chromosome, but not to extrachromosomally replicating lytic <jats:italic>P. aeruginosa<\/jats:italic> bacteriophage. We next assessed the ability of the more prevalent <jats:italic>Yersinia<\/jats:italic> subtype CRISPR regions to mediate resistance to bacteriophage infection or lysogeny by deleting the entire CRISPR region from sequenced strain UCBPP-PA14 and six clinical isolates. We found no change in CRISPR-mediated resistance to bacteriophage infection or lysogeny rate even for CRISPR with spacers 100\u200a% identical to a region of the infecting bacteriophage. Lastly, to show these CRISPR and <jats:italic>cas<\/jats:italic> genes were expressed and functional, we demonstrated production of small CRISPR RNAs. This work provides both the first examination to our knowledge of CRISPR regions within clinical <jats:italic>P. aeruginosa<\/jats:italic> isolates and a collection of defined CRISPR-positive and -negative strains for further CRISPR and <jats:italic>cas<\/jats:italic> gene studies.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Microbiology"],"is-referenced-by-count":89},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jmb.2010.11.019","title":["The Structure of the CRISPR-Associated Protein Csa3 Provides Insight into the Regulation of the CRISPR\/Cas System"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2011,1]]},"author":[{"given":"Nathanael G.","family":"Lintner","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Kenneth A.","family":"Frankel","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Susan E.","family":"Tsutakawa","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Donald L.","family":"Alsbury","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Val\u00e9rie","family"